What do they have to be wary of?

00:06:40.520 --> 00:06:41.810
AJA HAMMERLY: So that's so cool.

00:06:41.810 --> 00:06:43.010
At the top of the
show, actually, you

00:06:43.010 --> 00:06:45.052
said something about
natural language processing.

00:06:45.052 --> 00:06:47.715
Can you give me the 5,000-foot
view of what that is?

00:06:47.715 --> 00:06:48.590
DALE MARKOWITZ: Sure.

00:06:48.590 --> 00:06:51.410
Natural language processing
is the study of algorithms

00:06:51.410 --> 00:06:53.390
that deal with processing text.

00:06:53.390 --> 00:06:56.270
And usually today, that means
basically the intersection

00:06:56.270 --> 00:06:58.970
of machine learning
and text processing.

00:06:58.970 --> 00:07:00.470
AJA HAMMERLY: So
what kind of things

00:07:00.470 --> 00:07:03.375
are natural language processing
used for in the real world?

00:07:03.375 --> 00:07:05.000
DALE MARKOWITZ: So
in the world, people

00:07:05.000 --> 00:07:07.500
use natural language
processing for lots of things.

00:07:07.500 --> 00:07:11.120
So one really common use
case is you have lots of text

00:07:11.120 --> 00:07:15.530
from your customers posting
about your company on Twitter

00:07:15.530 --> 00:07:18.480
or filling out a feedback
form about your product.

00:07:18.480 --> 00:07:20.990
Or maybe you have a call center
where people are calling in

00:07:20.990 --> 00:07:24.230
to ask questions, and you use
something like Speech-To-Text

00:07:24.230 --> 00:07:26.690
to transcribe those
call center transcripts.

00:07:26.690 --> 00:07:28.190
And then, you want
to analyze to see

00:07:28.190 --> 00:07:29.630
what people were talking about.

00:07:29.630 --> 00:07:32.120
So one common natural
language processing problem

00:07:32.120 --> 00:07:33.710
is you have a lot
of text and we want

00:07:33.710 --> 00:07:35.970
to see what topics are
being talked about,

00:07:35.970 --> 00:07:40.070
like topic modeling, or sort
a bunch of pieces of text

00:07:40.070 --> 00:07:42.180
into categories.

00:07:42.180 --> 00:07:43.820
Another common task
you might imagine

00:07:43.820 --> 00:07:46.170
is something called
sentiment analysis.

00:07:46.170 --> 00:07:48.680
So this is about
what emotions people

00:07:48.680 --> 00:07:50.060
are expressing when they write.

00:07:50.060 --> 00:07:52.850
So are people saying positive
things or negative things

00:07:52.850 --> 00:07:54.545
about your product on Twitter?

00:07:54.545 --> 00:07:57.028
AJA HAMMERLY: That sounds
super cool and super powerful.

00:07:57.028 --> 00:07:58.820
JON FOUST: I want to
talk a little bit more

00:07:58.820 --> 00:08:02.270
about those people that don't
have degrees a little bit.

00:08:02.270 --> 00:08:03.770
I was kind of curious--

00:08:03.770 --> 00:08:06.608
we have people who
have degrees in ML,

00:08:06.608 --> 00:08:08.150
and their job is to
work with models.

00:08:08.150 --> 00:08:11.700
But those people without degrees
and they work on our models,

00:08:11.700 --> 00:08:15.830
creating models is a
very wishy-washy subject

00:08:15.830 --> 00:08:19.530
because you can create a model
that can be completely biased.

00:08:19.530 --> 00:08:21.260
How does someone
without a degree

00:08:21.260 --> 00:08:24.087
create a model that is deemed
unbiased in your opinion?

00:08:24.087 --> 00:08:25.670
DALE MARKOWITZ:
That's a good question

00:08:25.670 --> 00:08:28.280
because it is true that if you
are doing a normal software

00:08:28.280 --> 00:08:30.050
engineering task then--

00:08:30.050 --> 00:08:32.240
not all the time,
but a lot of time--

00:08:32.240 --> 00:08:34.880
you can immediately tell if
it's broken because your program

00:08:34.880 --> 00:08:36.918
crashes in a really
horrendous way.

00:08:36.918 --> 00:08:38.460
Unfortunately, with
machine learning,

00:08:38.460 --> 00:08:40.610
you can be working on
something for months,

00:08:40.610 --> 00:08:42.110
and then you put
it into production,

00:08:42.110 --> 00:08:44.600
and you don't even realize
it's broken for a while.

00:08:44.600 --> 00:08:46.850
And it's broken in a
way that is surprising

00:08:46.850 --> 00:08:49.130
or that was hard for
you to anticipate.

00:08:49.130 --> 00:08:51.890
Like, for example, a lot of
times we want to label things.

00:08:51.890 --> 00:08:54.380
So we have pictures
of dogs and cats,

00:08:54.380 --> 00:08:56.780
and we build a model that
automatically labels them

00:08:56.780 --> 00:08:57.900
as dogs or cats.

00:08:57.900 --> 00:08:59.900
So you might think that
a really good model just

00:08:59.900 --> 00:09:01.100
automates this process.

00:09:01.100 --> 00:09:03.800
But actually, a really
good model only correctly

00:09:03.800 --> 00:09:05.730
labels these things
most of the time.

00:09:05.730 --> 00:09:07.650
But all models make mistakes.

00:09:07.650 --> 00:09:09.440
So this is just a natural thing.

00:09:09.440 --> 00:09:11.150
And when you're
developing, first of all,

00:09:11.150 --> 00:09:12.792
you have to expect
this to happen,

00:09:12.792 --> 00:09:14.750
and you have to make sure
that the consequences

00:09:14.750 --> 00:09:17.030
of your model making a
mistake are not that bad.

00:09:17.030 --> 00:09:19.730
This is why I hope that most
of the people that I'm talking

00:09:19.730 --> 00:09:22.280
to that are trying out machine
learning for the first time

00:09:22.280 --> 00:09:25.310
are planning on deploying their
models in non-crucial settings,

00:09:25.310 --> 00:09:27.500
that they're not
building navigation

00:09:27.500 --> 00:09:31.700
systems for airplanes or
analyzing medical scans

00:09:31.700 --> 00:09:33.720
and then making life
or death decisions.

00:09:33.720 --> 00:09:36.470
So the first thing I think
is to not choose applications

00:09:36.470 --> 00:09:39.515
where the consequences of making
a mistake are really deadly.

00:09:39.515 --> 00:09:41.390
Then the next thing that
we would tell people

00:09:41.390 --> 00:09:43.640
is to consider the
different types of errors

00:09:43.640 --> 00:09:46.550
that a model can make because
there are different ways model

00:09:46.550 --> 00:09:47.240
can be wrong.

00:09:47.240 --> 00:09:49.610
For example, with the
cats and dogs case,

00:09:49.610 --> 00:09:53.450
you could label a cat as
a dog or a dog as a cat.

00:09:53.450 --> 00:09:56.150
Let's say that we're
looking at scans of lungs,

00:09:56.150 --> 00:09:59.930
and we want to see if we can
detect pneumonia automatically.

00:09:59.930 --> 00:10:03.345
Machine learning is very good
at understanding medical scans.

00:10:03.345 --> 00:10:04.970
Actually, a lot of
models we have today

00:10:04.970 --> 00:10:07.430
can even beat human
practitioners.

00:10:07.430 --> 00:10:09.327
But let's say we're
doing this task.

00:10:09.327 --> 00:10:10.910
So the first thing
that a model can do

00:10:10.910 --> 00:10:13.530
is it can give you
a false positive.

00:10:13.530 --> 00:10:16.268
So this would mean it
looks at a scan of lungs,

00:10:16.268 --> 00:10:18.560
and it thinks that you have
a pneumonia when you really

00:10:18.560 --> 00:10:19.220
don't.

00:10:19.220 --> 00:10:22.220
That error is
definitely horrifying,

00:10:22.220 --> 00:10:24.260
but maybe it's not
that bad because it

00:10:24.260 --> 00:10:26.690
means that you, I don't know,
spend more money on scans

00:10:26.690 --> 00:10:28.815
and doctors only to find
out that you didn't really

00:10:28.815 --> 00:10:29.455
have a problem.

00:10:29.455 --> 00:10:32.080
But then there's another type of
error called a false negative,

00:10:32.080 --> 00:10:33.580
where you really
did have pneumonia,

00:10:33.580 --> 00:10:35.393
but the model says
that you didn't.

00:10:35.393 --> 00:10:37.310
And that's much more
costly because now you've

00:10:37.310 --> 00:10:40.310
just overlooked a potentially
life threatening or very

00:10:40.310 --> 00:10:41.550
serious disease.

00:10:41.550 --> 00:10:43.190
So the next thing
for practitioners

00:10:43.190 --> 00:10:45.398
is to think about all these
different types of errors

00:10:45.398 --> 00:10:47.900
and understand what
you would do in each

00:10:47.900 --> 00:10:49.800
of these different cases.

00:10:49.800 --> 00:10:52.070
So another way that
models can be wrong

00:10:52.070 --> 00:10:53.985
is that they can
somehow introduce bias.

00:10:53.985 --> 00:10:55.860
And there are many ways
that this can happen,

00:10:55.860 --> 00:10:58.160
but usually it happens
because the examples

00:10:58.160 --> 00:11:00.530
that you've shown your
model, the training data,

00:11:00.530 --> 00:11:02.180
is in some way biased.

00:11:02.180 --> 00:11:04.227
So for example,
in my opinion, one

00:11:04.227 --> 00:11:06.560
of the worst ways that machine
learning has been applied

00:11:06.560 --> 00:11:09.060
is to something like
predictive policing.

00:11:09.060 --> 00:11:11.650
So you want to somehow
collect data about people

00:11:11.650 --> 00:11:13.400
and predict who's
likely to commit a crime

00:11:13.400 --> 00:11:15.582
or who's likely to
re-commit a crime.

00:11:15.582 --> 00:11:17.040
The problem here
is that the people

00:11:17.040 --> 00:11:20.030
that get pulled over for crimes
in the first place is biased.

00:11:20.030 --> 00:11:23.348
And in fact, years and years of
policing has innate human bias.

00:11:23.348 --> 00:11:24.890
So if you train a
model on that data,

00:11:24.890 --> 00:11:27.380
you're just going to
reflect those biases.

00:11:27.380 --> 00:11:30.800
The right solution here is
to choose your data sets

00:11:30.800 --> 00:11:33.657
carefully, so that they reflect
all of the different groups

00:11:33.657 --> 00:11:35.240
that you can think
of that there might

00:11:35.240 --> 00:11:36.550
be a difference in performance.

00:11:36.550 --> 00:11:39.380
So let's say you're training
a vision model to identify

00:11:39.380 --> 00:11:40.615
people's faces.

00:11:40.615 --> 00:11:42.740
You want to make sure that
your data set has people

00:11:42.740 --> 00:11:46.640
of all different races, all
different ages, sexes, outfits,

00:11:46.640 --> 00:11:47.500
et cetera.

00:11:47.500 --> 00:11:49.250
And if you're really
concerned about this,

00:11:49.250 --> 00:11:51.770
then you want to look at
how well the model does

00:11:51.770 --> 00:11:53.270
specifically on these groups.

00:11:53.270 --> 00:11:54.890
So break your data into
these different groups,

00:11:54.890 --> 00:11:56.557
and make sure that
performance is pretty

00:11:56.557 --> 00:11:57.848
similar across all of them.

00:11:57.848 --> 00:12:00.140
This is something we're really
interested in at Google.

00:12:00.140 --> 00:12:02.360
It's something that we're really
spending a lot of time thinking

00:12:02.360 --> 00:12:04.402
about how we can make this
more clear to the user

00:12:04.402 --> 00:12:06.319
and how we can integrate
it into our products.

00:12:06.319 --> 00:12:08.152
AJA HAMMERLY: So do you
have any guidelines?

00:12:08.152 --> 00:12:10.250
I mean, you mentioned
looking at all the ways

00:12:10.250 --> 00:12:11.160
that it can go wrong.

00:12:11.160 --> 00:12:12.350
Do you have any
guidelines for folks

00:12:12.350 --> 00:12:14.100
who are just getting
into machine learning

00:12:14.100 --> 00:12:16.670
or aren't necessarily
data scientists by trade

00:12:16.670 --> 00:12:18.410
but are using some
of the products that

00:12:18.410 --> 00:12:19.618
make machine learning easier?

00:12:19.618 --> 00:12:21.035
What kinds of stuff
should they be

00:12:21.035 --> 00:12:23.202
thinking about when they're
working on their models?

00:12:23.202 --> 00:12:24.077
DALE MARKOWITZ: Sure.

00:12:24.077 --> 00:12:25.830
So when you first
learn machine learning,

00:12:25.830 --> 00:12:27.830
you'll have to
learn how to parse

00:12:27.830 --> 00:12:30.050
a bunch of different
metrics to just baseline

00:12:30.050 --> 00:12:33.140
evaluate how well the model is
doing-- precision and recall.

00:12:33.140 --> 00:12:35.293
I want to talk about
that because you probably

00:12:35.293 --> 00:12:36.710
have to read a
blog post and spend

00:12:36.710 --> 00:12:39.260
a couple of minutes thinking
about it before it really

00:12:39.260 --> 00:12:40.400
sinks in.

00:12:40.400 --> 00:12:42.710
But the strongest advice
that I have probably

00:12:42.710 --> 00:12:45.800
is that you have this data
that use to train a model.

00:12:45.800 --> 00:12:48.800
And then, you try to somehow
deploy it into production

00:12:48.800 --> 00:12:50.630
to make predictions on the fly.

00:12:50.630 --> 00:12:52.670
And a lot of times,
people get caught up

00:12:52.670 --> 00:12:55.370
on the data set that they use
to train the model doesn't

00:12:55.370 --> 00:12:58.400
actually reflect the data that
they're making predictions on.

00:12:58.400 --> 00:13:00.470
So maybe I use a
bunch of stock photos

00:13:00.470 --> 00:13:02.610
to train a clothing classifier.

00:13:02.610 --> 00:13:04.490
But then, when I actually
use my clothing app

00:13:04.490 --> 00:13:06.990
and people are taking pictures
of themselves, well suddenly,

00:13:06.990 --> 00:13:09.300
those photos look a lot
different than stock photos.

00:13:09.300 --> 00:13:11.330
So that's a major
source of error.

00:13:11.330 --> 00:13:14.360
And also, sometimes, the
distribution of data can shift.

00:13:14.360 --> 00:13:16.670
So in the beginning, your
model works really well.

00:13:16.670 --> 00:13:18.470
And then style trends change.

00:13:18.470 --> 00:13:20.780
And suddenly, it doesn't
recognize that tutus are in

00:13:20.780 --> 00:13:21.650
or something.

00:13:21.650 --> 00:13:24.320
I would really advise that when
you deploy a model, before you

00:13:24.320 --> 00:13:27.763
let it go into the wild, you
watch how it performs on data.

00:13:27.763 --> 00:13:29.180
You don't let it
make predictions,

00:13:29.180 --> 00:13:31.700
but you sort of simulate what
predictions it would make.

00:13:31.700 --> 00:13:33.548
And make sure that
you're OK with those.

00:13:33.548 --> 00:13:35.090
And also, understand
that you do have

00:13:35.090 --> 00:13:38.090
to be continually monitoring
your model because of the fact

00:13:38.090 --> 00:13:40.100
that the real
world could change.

00:13:40.100 --> 00:13:41.600
AJA HAMMERLY: So
it sounds like when

00:13:41.600 --> 00:13:42.730
you're using machine
learning, you

00:13:42.730 --> 00:13:44.030
need to constantly
be on top of it

00:13:44.030 --> 00:13:45.655
to make sure that
it's telling you what

00:13:45.655 --> 00:13:46.780
you think it's telling you?

00:13:46.780 --> 00:13:48.655
DALE MARKOWITZ: To make
sure it's telling you

00:13:48.655 --> 00:13:50.130
what you want it
to be telling you.

00:13:50.130 --> 00:13:50.838
AJA HAMMERLY: OK.

00:13:50.838 --> 00:13:52.340
JON FOUST: So I'm
kind of curious.

00:13:52.340 --> 00:13:54.100
Since you have to
continue monitoring it,

00:13:54.100 --> 00:13:56.100
you can't anticipate how
your users are actually

00:13:56.100 --> 00:13:57.840
going to use your models.

00:13:57.840 --> 00:14:00.690
How long do you think it
actually takes for you to--

00:14:00.690 --> 00:14:03.600
the word "perfect" is going to
be in air quotes right now--

00:14:03.600 --> 00:14:06.720
but how long do you think it
takes to create an accepted,

00:14:06.720 --> 00:14:08.155
fully-functioning model?

00:14:08.155 --> 00:14:09.780
DALE MARKOWITZ: It
depends on the task.

00:14:09.780 --> 00:14:13.680
It depends on your standards for
how good it should be working.

00:14:13.680 --> 00:14:16.050
For example, there's
also a lot of thought

00:14:16.050 --> 00:14:20.910
around designing interfaces so
that users that are interacting

00:14:20.910 --> 00:14:24.570
with machine learning models are
aware that there's going to be

00:14:24.570 --> 00:14:26.153
some probabilistic component.

00:14:26.153 --> 00:14:27.570
JON FOUST: So I'm
kind of curious.

00:14:27.570 --> 00:14:30.150
When do you think it is
actually acceptable to use ML?

00:14:30.150 --> 00:14:32.100
Because you can use ML
for a bunch of things

00:14:32.100 --> 00:14:33.540
to solve a bunch of issues.

00:14:33.540 --> 00:14:35.640
And you can probably
use ML to do things

00:14:35.640 --> 00:14:37.340
that probably aren't ethical.

00:14:37.340 --> 00:14:39.450
But when do you find it
acceptable to use ML?

00:14:39.450 --> 00:14:40.950
DALE MARKOWITZ:
Like I said, I think

00:14:40.950 --> 00:14:42.840
that there are lots of cases.

00:14:42.840 --> 00:14:45.630
I wouldn't use machine learning
for anything life threatening.

00:14:45.630 --> 00:14:48.270
I wouldn't use machine learning
for anything that determines

00:14:48.270 --> 00:14:50.070
the fate of people's lives.

00:14:50.070 --> 00:14:54.090
There are sort of lots of, I
think, low-hanging tasks that--

00:14:54.090 --> 00:14:55.710
one of my colleagues
described machine

00:14:55.710 --> 00:14:58.762
learning as being kind of a
little bit of a dumb intern.

00:14:58.762 --> 00:14:59.720
[LAUGHTER AND APPLAUSE]

00:14:59.720 --> 00:15:01.640
It takes a lot of
training, and it can only

00:15:01.640 --> 00:15:03.570
do very menial
tasks, but there are

00:15:03.570 --> 00:15:05.813
lots of little tasks like
that in the world that

00:15:05.813 --> 00:15:08.355
have yet to be automated that
it would be great if they were.

00:15:09.180 --> 00:15:11.377
Just for example,
journalists, they

00:15:11.377 --> 00:15:12.960
interview people for
stories, and they

00:15:12.960 --> 00:15:16.808
collect hours and hours of
transcripts, and they sit down

00:15:16.808 --> 00:15:17.850
and they transcribe them.

00:15:17.850 --> 00:15:19.440
So journalists, a
lot of their time

00:15:19.440 --> 00:15:22.037
is spent just literally
copying an audio file to text.

00:15:22.037 --> 00:15:24.370
That's something that we could
do with machine learning,

00:15:24.370 --> 00:15:26.828
and it's something that I don't
think anyone would complain

00:15:26.828 --> 00:15:27.900
about as an application.

00:15:27.900 --> 00:15:29.855
AJA HAMMERLY: That
makes a lot of sense.

00:15:29.855 --> 00:15:31.980
So you mentioned that you're
into natural language.

00:15:31.980 --> 00:15:34.740
What kind of natural language
tools are available on GCP?

00:15:34.740 --> 00:15:37.630
Because I've played a little
bit with some of the tools,

00:15:37.630 --> 00:15:40.230
trying to detect if one of
my friends was making a pun

00:15:40.230 --> 00:15:42.637
or not because he is a
very punny human being.

00:15:42.637 --> 00:15:44.220
But I feel like
there's a lot of tools

00:15:44.220 --> 00:15:45.580
that I don't even know about.

00:15:45.580 --> 00:15:47.955
So what kinds of tools do we
have around natural language

00:15:47.955 --> 00:15:48.455
on GCP?

00:15:48.455 --> 00:15:49.872
DALE MARKOWITZ:
So glad you asked.

00:15:49.872 --> 00:15:52.830
With machine learning, it goes
sort of like on this spectrum

00:15:52.830 --> 00:15:55.440
of easy-to-get-started
to advanced.

00:15:55.440 --> 00:15:57.480
So the easiest tools
that you could use

00:15:57.480 --> 00:16:00.160
would be something like
the Natural Language API.

00:16:00.160 --> 00:16:02.350
So this is an API
used like any other.

00:16:02.350 --> 00:16:06.570
The input is text, and it can
do things like detect sentiment,

00:16:06.570 --> 00:16:09.220
detect entities,
like people's names,

00:16:09.220 --> 00:16:12.540
places, prices, dates,
addresses, and so on.

00:16:12.540 --> 00:16:15.290
It can also detect
the subject of text.

00:16:15.290 --> 00:16:18.040
So if you uploaded a "New York
Times" article about politics,

00:16:18.040 --> 00:16:21.580
it could detect that from an
article about sports and so on.

00:16:21.580 --> 00:16:24.240
So that's the easiest entry
way into natural language

00:16:24.240 --> 00:16:25.170
processing.

00:16:25.170 --> 00:16:26.670
Then a step-- I
wouldn't say it's

00:16:26.670 --> 00:16:29.430
harder, but maybe
more sophisticated

00:16:29.430 --> 00:16:32.160
is a tool like AutoML
Natural Language.

00:16:32.160 --> 00:16:34.590
It's a tool that allows
you to train your own model

00:16:34.590 --> 00:16:37.320
on your own data from scratch,
and you do this with a GUI,

00:16:37.320 --> 00:16:39.750
and it's quite easy to use.

00:16:39.750 --> 00:16:41.520
And there are a bunch
of different models

00:16:41.520 --> 00:16:43.480
that we can build for text.

00:16:43.480 --> 00:16:45.992
So the first one is the
classification model.

00:16:45.992 --> 00:16:47.700
We worked with a recipe
company, and they

00:16:47.700 --> 00:16:50.760
wanted to be able to
categorize their recipes into--

00:16:50.760 --> 00:16:54.043
I don't know-- category like
southern food, Mexican food.

00:16:54.043 --> 00:16:55.710
But you could upload
a bunch of recipes,

00:16:55.710 --> 00:16:57.870
have humans label them,
and then train a model

00:16:57.870 --> 00:16:59.350
to do this automatically.

00:16:59.350 --> 00:17:00.930
So that's classification.

00:17:00.930 --> 00:17:03.700
You can also build a
custom sentiment model.

00:17:03.700 --> 00:17:05.369
So again, this is
about emotions.

00:17:05.369 --> 00:17:08.760
Is this piece of text expressing
positive or negative feelings?

00:17:08.760 --> 00:17:11.400
And within AutoML, you
can sort of fine tune

00:17:11.400 --> 00:17:13.930
this model to your
own specific data.

00:17:13.930 --> 00:17:17.130
So for example, the
Natural Language API

00:17:17.130 --> 00:17:21.060
can tell that if I say,
I hate oranges, that I'm

00:17:21.060 --> 00:17:22.770
expressing negative sentiment.

00:17:22.770 --> 00:17:27.060
But if I said instead, there
is no leg room in coach,

00:17:27.060 --> 00:17:30.780
then a model might not know if
that is a bad thing because leg

00:17:30.780 --> 00:17:34.415
room is such a specific niche
thing to flying on an airplane.

00:17:34.415 --> 00:17:35.790
So you might train
a custom model

00:17:35.790 --> 00:17:39.510
that's more aware of your
specific industry terminology.

00:17:39.510 --> 00:17:42.420
Also, a new AutoML feature
that was released recently

00:17:42.420 --> 00:17:44.760
was Custom Entity Extraction.

00:17:44.760 --> 00:17:47.010
So this is kind of
like classification,

00:17:47.010 --> 00:17:50.650
but you can use it to
identify words within text.

00:17:50.650 --> 00:17:53.820
So for example, you
could upload a W-4

00:17:53.820 --> 00:17:56.280
and train a model to extract
all of the form fields

00:17:56.280 --> 00:17:57.500
that you'd filled out.

00:17:57.500 --> 00:17:59.345
I built a model to
classify patents.

00:17:59.345 --> 00:18:01.750
So you have a bunch of
patent applications as PDFs,

00:18:01.750 --> 00:18:03.750
and you identify different
fields in the patent.

00:18:03.750 --> 00:18:05.610
So that's AutoML.

00:18:05.610 --> 00:18:07.380
And then there are
lots of options

00:18:07.380 --> 00:18:09.977
for people that are more sort
of savvy with data science.

00:18:09.977 --> 00:18:12.060
So if you're willing to
get dirty with TensorFlow,

00:18:12.060 --> 00:18:14.435
then types of natural language
models you could build out

00:18:14.435 --> 00:18:16.520
are endless, and we have
lots of infrastructure

00:18:16.520 --> 00:18:18.900
for helping you train
and deploy those models.

00:18:18.900 --> 00:18:19.710
AJA HAMMERLY: Cool.

00:18:19.710 --> 00:18:20.710
JON FOUST: So Dale,
you also mentioned

00:18:20.710 --> 00:18:21.820
you work in translation.

00:18:21.820 --> 00:18:24.000
So is there anything
new that's come about

00:18:24.000 --> 00:18:25.558
with the Translation API?

00:18:25.558 --> 00:18:26.850
DALE MARKOWITZ: Yeah, actually.

00:18:26.850 --> 00:18:29.470
We just recently
released some updates.

00:18:29.470 --> 00:18:31.800
So if you've never used
the Translation API,

00:18:31.800 --> 00:18:34.750
it's sort of like Google
Translate but in API format.

00:18:34.750 --> 00:18:37.570
So you can use our
models within your app.

00:18:37.570 --> 00:18:40.210
So the Translate API
works, I think, very well.

00:18:40.210 --> 00:18:42.060
However, there are
some cases where it

00:18:42.060 --> 00:18:43.560
doesn't do as great of a job.

00:18:43.560 --> 00:18:47.370
For example, Google Cloud has
lots of documentation online

00:18:47.370 --> 00:18:49.770
and lots of really
specific terminology,

00:18:49.770 --> 00:18:53.040
like Kubernetes, or Cloud
Run, or TensorFlow, or all

00:18:53.040 --> 00:18:56.440
of this stuff that may be
a generic translation model

00:18:56.440 --> 00:18:58.300
wouldn't have seen before.

00:18:58.300 --> 00:18:59.800
So we have a couple
of features that

00:18:59.800 --> 00:19:02.110
can help you improve
your translations

00:19:02.110 --> 00:19:04.120
for this sort of custom domain.

00:19:04.120 --> 00:19:06.880
One thing, if you want to build
a really high quality model,

00:19:06.880 --> 00:19:08.920
is AutoML translate.

00:19:08.920 --> 00:19:11.080
So I just talked about
AutoML Natural Language,

00:19:11.080 --> 00:19:15.250
but AutoML Translate allows you
to upload lots of translations

00:19:15.250 --> 00:19:17.600
that you've done on
whatever your data set is.

00:19:17.600 --> 00:19:19.870
So for example, with
the GCP documentation,

00:19:19.870 --> 00:19:21.970
I might upload
English documentation

00:19:21.970 --> 00:19:24.040
and Japanese documentation.

00:19:24.040 --> 00:19:27.340
And then, AutoML will train
a model that automatically

00:19:27.340 --> 00:19:29.410
does those translations
that includes

00:19:29.410 --> 00:19:32.410
this knowledge it learns from
your own specific data set.

00:19:32.410 --> 00:19:34.660
So this builds very
accurate models,

00:19:34.660 --> 00:19:37.750
but it takes a lot of data
to train a model like this.

00:19:37.750 --> 00:19:39.880
But we also just
released a new feature

00:19:39.880 --> 00:19:42.730
to allow you to customize
your translations just

00:19:42.730 --> 00:19:44.470
by using the API.

00:19:44.470 --> 00:19:46.930
And that's this feature
called Glossary.

00:19:46.930 --> 00:19:48.460
So the idea is--

00:19:48.460 --> 00:19:51.730
let's say that I'm going to
translate lots of things that

00:19:51.730 --> 00:19:53.710
also mention Google,
but there are some words

00:19:53.710 --> 00:19:55.940
that I know exactly how I
want them to be translated.

00:19:55.940 --> 00:19:58.180
So I want Kubernetes to be
translated into Japanese

00:19:58.180 --> 00:20:00.280
in this very specific way
that is not the default

00:20:00.280 --> 00:20:02.410
way that the
Translation API does it.

00:20:02.410 --> 00:20:04.210
The new feature is
called Glossary.

00:20:04.210 --> 00:20:07.438
So it allows us to upload
a list of word pairs

00:20:07.438 --> 00:20:09.730
in one language and then the
one you're translating to,

00:20:09.730 --> 00:20:12.850
so that the API will, before
it does any translation,

00:20:12.850 --> 00:20:15.040
just make these one-for-one
replacements using

00:20:15.040 --> 00:20:16.390
your preferred translation.

00:20:16.390 --> 00:20:18.598
And then, it will translate
the rest of the sentence.

00:20:18.598 --> 00:20:20.432
AJA HAMMERLY: That's
actually super powerful

00:20:20.432 --> 00:20:22.060
because I don't know
of any domain that

00:20:22.060 --> 00:20:25.840
doesn't have domain-specific
vocabulary or uses

00:20:25.840 --> 00:20:28.810
words in ways that normal
people don't use words.

00:20:28.810 --> 00:20:31.750
And sometimes, you
have terms that

00:20:31.750 --> 00:20:34.030
need to be translated
in very specific ways.

00:20:34.030 --> 00:20:35.170
So that's super cool.

00:20:35.170 --> 00:20:37.900
I can imagine a lot of people
would get use out of that.

00:20:37.900 --> 00:20:38.440
DALE MARKOWITZ:
Yeah, we actually

00:20:38.440 --> 00:20:39.970
think about it a
lot for translating

00:20:39.970 --> 00:20:41.028
our own documentation.

00:20:41.028 --> 00:20:43.570
AJA HAMMERLY: Best use cases
are the ones that we experience,

00:20:43.570 --> 00:20:44.980
too.

00:20:44.980 --> 00:20:46.300
Sometimes.

00:20:46.300 --> 00:20:47.562
That's cool.

00:20:47.562 --> 00:20:50.020
JON FOUST: So Dale, you and I
have been really good friends

00:20:50.020 --> 00:20:51.910
in the office, and we've
been talking about a couple

00:20:51.910 --> 00:20:53.493
of things that you've
been working on.

00:20:53.493 --> 00:20:56.350
So you mentioned that you
work on this project called

00:20:56.350 --> 00:20:57.640
Google News Lab.

00:20:57.640 --> 00:21:00.498
So I'm curious to learn a
little bit more about it,

00:21:00.498 --> 00:21:02.290
and I'm pretty sure
our listeners are, too.

00:21:02.290 --> 00:21:03.760
DALE MARKOWITZ: Yeah, sure.

00:21:03.760 --> 00:21:07.240
So ever since I joined Google,
I knew about the Google News Lab

00:21:07.240 --> 00:21:09.560
and thought it sounded
really awesome.

00:21:09.560 --> 00:21:12.790
It's this team here that
works with newsrooms

00:21:12.790 --> 00:21:16.493
to help them advance
journalism through technology.

00:21:16.493 --> 00:21:18.160
So they'll work with
different newsrooms

00:21:18.160 --> 00:21:20.620
and help them build out
technology that helps

00:21:20.620 --> 00:21:21.922
with investigative reporting.

00:21:21.922 --> 00:21:24.130
And what I do with them is
I'm sort of like a machine

00:21:24.130 --> 00:21:25.360
learning consultant.

00:21:25.360 --> 00:21:26.830
So I'll talk to
newsrooms about how

00:21:26.830 --> 00:21:29.110
they might use machine
learning in a project

00:21:29.110 --> 00:21:30.280
or something like this.

00:21:30.280 --> 00:21:31.900
AJA HAMMERLY: Do you have any
examples you can tell us about?

00:21:31.900 --> 00:21:34.100
Because I'm not sure I can
imagine what that looks like.

00:21:34.100 --> 00:21:35.225
DALE MARKOWITZ: Yeah, sure.

00:21:35.225 --> 00:21:37.720
I can tell you about a project
that we just worked on.

00:21:37.720 --> 00:21:40.510
So I recently finished
up working on a project

00:21:40.510 --> 00:21:43.900
with a newspaper in Mexico
called "El Universal."

00:21:43.900 --> 00:21:47.050
They wanted to tackle a really
complicated issue, which

00:21:47.050 --> 00:21:50.640
is that in Mexico, there
is a lot of homicides.

00:21:50.640 --> 00:21:54.340
And unfortunately, some areas
are so dangerous that reporters

00:21:54.340 --> 00:21:56.530
can't even cover
homicides because they

00:21:56.530 --> 00:21:58.780
are at risk in doing so.

00:21:58.780 --> 00:22:01.150
These we call news
deserts, the areas that

00:22:01.150 --> 00:22:04.130
are too dangerous to report on.

00:22:04.130 --> 00:22:06.160
So we worked with this
newspaper to build

00:22:06.160 --> 00:22:09.430
a system that could identify
these places where stories

00:22:09.430 --> 00:22:11.410
weren't written on crimes.

00:22:11.410 --> 00:22:15.070
So to do that, Google News
has this comprehensive stream

00:22:15.070 --> 00:22:17.390
of news stories written
all around the world.

00:22:17.390 --> 00:22:22.240
So we looked at all of the
news stories written in Mexico,

00:22:22.240 --> 00:22:23.920
and we whittled it
down to stories that

00:22:23.920 --> 00:22:26.050
were written about homicides.

00:22:26.050 --> 00:22:28.270
And then, we sort of
used machine learning,

00:22:28.270 --> 00:22:30.220
and we used AutoML,
actually, to figure out

00:22:30.220 --> 00:22:33.247
which of those stories would
be relevant for coverage.

00:22:33.247 --> 00:22:35.080
So they were talking
about a crime committed

00:22:35.080 --> 00:22:37.690
on a specific day in
a specific region.

00:22:37.690 --> 00:22:40.323
We were able to sort of use
this to build this map of places

00:22:40.323 --> 00:22:41.740
where crimes were
being committed,

00:22:41.740 --> 00:22:43.360
but they weren't
being reported about.

00:22:43.360 --> 00:22:46.340
So it's a pretty heavy,
sobering subject,

00:22:46.340 --> 00:22:48.972
but it was needed to be able to
help out with this reporting.

00:22:48.972 --> 00:22:50.680
JON FOUST: This seems
really interesting.

00:22:50.680 --> 00:22:52.263
You've mentioned
that you used AutoML,

00:22:52.263 --> 00:22:54.910
but specifically, what
product in AutoML?

00:22:54.910 --> 00:22:56.555
Like, NLP, Translation?

00:22:56.555 --> 00:22:58.930
DALE MARKOWITZ: Yeah, we used
Natural Language Processing

00:22:58.930 --> 00:23:00.670
API and AutoML.

00:23:00.670 --> 00:23:04.420
So we used the API to do
things like extract locations

00:23:04.420 --> 00:23:06.170
mentioned in news stories.

00:23:06.170 --> 00:23:09.880
And then, we used AutoML to
build a classifier that figured

00:23:09.880 --> 00:23:13.420
out which articles were relevant
for our reporting versus which

00:23:13.420 --> 00:23:14.200
weren't.

00:23:14.200 --> 00:23:15.825
AJA HAMMERLY: You
said that it's heavy,

00:23:15.825 --> 00:23:17.742
but crime reporting is
super, super valuable.

00:23:17.742 --> 00:23:20.200
People need to know what's
going on in their neighborhoods.

00:23:20.200 --> 00:23:22.450
And I wish that this wasn't
a product that was needed,

00:23:22.450 --> 00:23:24.158
but at the same time,
it sounds like it's

00:23:24.158 --> 00:23:26.080
solving a real problem
to ensure that people

00:23:26.080 --> 00:23:28.750
can get news relevant
to them in spite of what

00:23:28.750 --> 00:23:30.130
may be going on around them.

00:23:30.130 --> 00:23:31.180
That's super cool.

00:23:31.180 --> 00:23:32.497
DALE MARKOWITZ: I hope so.

00:23:32.497 --> 00:23:34.580
AJA HAMMERLY: So we're
almost running out of time,

00:23:34.580 --> 00:23:36.610
but is there anything
that we haven't asked you

00:23:36.610 --> 00:23:38.090
about that you
want to talk about

00:23:38.090 --> 00:23:40.720
or that you think our listeners
would be interested in?

00:23:40.720 --> 00:23:41.110
DALE MARKOWITZ: Oh, yeah.

00:23:41.110 --> 00:23:42.520
Actually, there is one thing.

00:23:42.520 --> 00:23:45.070
I want to talk about my
favorite service on GCP

00:23:45.070 --> 00:23:46.670
that I feel like
is kind of hidden

00:23:46.670 --> 00:23:48.500
and people don't know exists.

00:23:48.500 --> 00:23:50.440
But if you are in
machine learning,

00:23:50.440 --> 00:23:51.690
it's a super useful tool.

00:23:51.690 --> 00:23:54.050
It's probably the
biggest unblocker.

00:23:54.050 --> 00:23:56.330
Our new Data Labeling Service.

00:23:56.330 --> 00:23:59.010
So the hardest part of machine
learning, in my opinion,

00:23:59.010 --> 00:24:01.275
is not learning what a neural
network is, or accuracy,

00:24:01.275 --> 00:24:02.150
or all of this stuff.

00:24:02.150 --> 00:24:04.190
It's actually finding a
labeled data set that's

00:24:04.190 --> 00:24:05.720
appropriate for your task.

00:24:05.720 --> 00:24:08.870
So you can go on Kaggle and try
to find one that's suitable,

00:24:08.870 --> 00:24:11.523
but if you want to, for example,
classify your own company's

00:24:11.523 --> 00:24:13.940
internal documents, then for
training and machine learning

00:24:13.940 --> 00:24:15.740
models, you need to
label those documents.

00:24:15.740 --> 00:24:18.032
And sometimes, you need to
create hundreds or thousands

00:24:18.032 --> 00:24:20.510
of labeled examples, which
means that you need to employ

00:24:20.510 --> 00:24:22.490
a lot of interns probably.

00:24:22.490 --> 00:24:23.480
Except until now.

00:24:23.480 --> 00:24:26.390
Because now, you can use
Google's Data Labeling Service.

00:24:26.390 --> 00:24:27.980
You basically write
up a description

00:24:27.980 --> 00:24:29.897
of how you want your
data labeled, you send it

00:24:29.897 --> 00:24:31.880
to the service, and then,
in a couple of days,

00:24:31.880 --> 00:24:34.980
the data set comes back
labeled by human beings.

00:24:34.980 --> 00:24:37.928
And it is designed to work
easily with all of our AutoMLs.

00:24:37.928 --> 00:24:39.470
So you can create
a data set and then

00:24:39.470 --> 00:24:42.590
immediately plug the output
into AutoML to build a model.

00:24:42.590 --> 00:24:44.090
So that's my favorite new thing.

00:24:44.090 --> 00:24:46.250
AJA HAMMERLY: That's
awesome, really powerful

00:24:46.250 --> 00:24:49.460
for folks who may not have the
expertise or experience to go

00:24:49.460 --> 00:24:51.140
find the data sets
that they need,

00:24:51.140 --> 00:24:53.810
or maybe their data set is
proprietary for some reason

00:24:53.810 --> 00:24:54.410
or another.

00:24:54.410 --> 00:24:54.945
That's cool.

00:24:54.945 --> 00:24:55.820
DALE MARKOWITZ: Yeah.

00:24:55.820 --> 00:24:57.290
JON FOUST: So
Dale, really quick,

00:24:57.290 --> 00:25:00.380
