00:25:13.655 --> 00:25:16.640
Like, for example, I used
AutoML to predict which posts

00:25:16.640 --> 00:25:18.260
would be top posts on Reddit.

00:25:18.260 --> 00:25:20.660
That's a really hard task,
as you might imagine,

00:25:20.660 --> 00:25:22.070
but it did a pretty good job.

00:25:22.070 --> 00:25:25.100
Or predict, for example,
whether a comment that somebody

00:25:25.100 --> 00:25:27.270
writes on a forum is toxic.

00:25:27.270 --> 00:25:29.917
I think my favorite thing is
that once you have a data set,

00:25:29.917 --> 00:25:31.250
it's very easy to build a model.

00:25:31.250 --> 00:25:34.400
So I'm always sort of trying
to see if I can predict things

00:25:34.400 --> 00:25:35.480
with AutoML.

00:25:35.480 --> 00:25:36.290
JON FOUST: That's
awesome because you

00:25:36.290 --> 00:25:37.957
can imagine a lot of
people go on forums

00:25:37.957 --> 00:25:39.725
or they read
comments on YouTube.

00:25:39.725 --> 00:25:41.100
And you can imagine
a lot of them

00:25:41.100 --> 00:25:43.100
are probably deemed like
offensive or something.

00:25:43.100 --> 00:25:46.310
So if you can use
NLP to actually scour

00:25:46.310 --> 00:25:50.210
the millions of comments,
possibly either censor or even

00:25:50.210 --> 00:25:52.760
complete erase those
type of comments,

00:25:52.760 --> 00:25:54.315
it builds for a
better community.

00:25:54.315 --> 00:25:55.190
DALE MARKOWITZ: Yeah.

00:25:55.190 --> 00:25:57.380
Also, one of my favorite
things that somebody built

00:25:57.380 --> 00:25:58.830
is something that
you built, Jon.

00:25:58.830 --> 00:26:00.198
Can I talk about it?

00:26:00.198 --> 00:26:01.115
JON FOUST: Yeah, sure.

00:26:01.115 --> 00:26:03.860
DALE MARKOWITZ: So Jon
